INSERT INTO paper_sections (id,paper_id,position,kind,title,instructions,paragraphs_json,study_paragraphs_json) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3','cet4-2024-06-2',2,'matching','长篇阅读（36–45）· How to better work towards long-term goals','In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2.','["[A] Hal Hershfield, a psychologist at the UCLA Anderson School of Management, wanted to know why people weren''t saving for retirement. Across the board, people are living longer. Logically, they''ll need more money to live comfortably in their post-work years. And yet, savings rates in the U.S. have gone down in recent decades, not up.","[B] To help explain this seemingly irrational behavior, Hershfield and his team scanned the brains of study participants while asking them to what degree various traits (特征)—like “honorable” or “funny”—applied to their current self, their future self, a current other, or a future other. As participants answered, Hershfield''s team recorded which parts of their brains lit up. Unsurprisingly, people''s brains were most active when thinking about their current selves and least active when thinking about a current other. But the team found that participants'' brain activity while considering their future selves more closely resembled their brain activity while thinking about a current other rather than the current self.","[C] Put in practical terms, when thinking of yourself in a month or a year or a decade, your brain registers that person in ways similar to how it would register Taylor Swift or the mailman. Understood in that way, saving for retirement is the equivalent of giving money away to someone else entirely","[D] In light of Hershfield''s study, one simple question arises: Is it possible to make our present selves give a damn about our future selves? The answers are anything but simple.","[E] Seen through the lens of the present self versus the future self, our self-defeating actions—like choosing to watch television rather than go to the gym—suddenly make perfect sense. We get to enjoy the very concrete, immediate benefits of our actions while someone else (namely, our future selves) suffers the hypothetical (假设的), far-off consequences. As a result, the decisions we make for our present selves often look very different from our decisions for our future selves. We believe that tomorrow. will be different. We believe that we will be different tomorrow; but in doing so, we prioritize our current mood over the consequences of our inaction for the future self.","[F] Understanding our procrastination (拖延) through the lens of the present and future selves, we''re left with three possible solutions: The first is to force your future self to do whatever your present self doesn''t want to do. The second is to convince your present self that your future self is, in fact, still you. If the central problem is that we think of our future selves as other people, it follows that trying to identify more closely with our future selves will encourage us to make better long-term decisions.","[G] In a follow-up study, Hershfield wanted to explore ways to bridge the disconnect between the present and future selves and encourage people to save more for retirement. He and his team took photos of study participants, and then used image processing to visually age their faces. Participants were then placed in a virtual reality setting where they could look into a mirror and see their aged selves looking back at them. Participants who saw their aged selves said they would save 30% more of their salary for retirement than the control group.","[H] Whatever your long-term goals may be—getting in better shape, launching your own business, writing a book—thinking about your deadline in terms of days rather than months or years can help you wrap your mind around how close the future really is.","[I] The third solution is to forget about your future self and use your present self''s love of instant gratifcation (满足感) to your advantage. While the two tactics (手段) above can be effective in making better long-term choices, in the end, you''re still struggling against human nature. Our brains are hard-wired for instant gratification. Instead of fighting your present self''s need for immediate rewards, why not use it to your advantage? When most of us set goals, we focus on long-term results we want to see— e.g., losing weight, getting a promotion, retiring in comfort, etc. While those visions of our future selves can be inspiring, when it comes to actually doing the day-to-day work, it may be more effective to reframe activities in terms of their immediate, or at least very near-term, rewards.","[J] Take writing this article, for instance. It''s easy for me to imagine how amazing it will feel at the end of the workday to have this article done. This isn''t just my opinion. Research partners Kaitlin Woolley of Cornell University and Ayelet Fishbach of the University of Chicago have made a career out of studying the differences between the goals that people achieve and the ones that fall to the wayside","[K] “In one study, we asked people online about the goals they set at the beginning of the year. Most people set goals to achieve delayed, long-term benefits, such as career advancement, debt repayment, or improved health. We asked these individuals how enjoyable it was to pursue their goal, as well as how important their goal was. We also asked whether they were still working on their goals two months after setting them. We found that enjoyment predicted people''s goal persistence two months after setting the goal far more than how important they rated their goal to be,” Woolley said.","[L] This pattern held true across a wide variety of goals from exercising to studying to eating healthier foods For example, people ate 50% more of a healthy food when directed to focus on the good taste rather than the long-term health benefits. Other studies have shown a greater uptake of exercise in people who were told to think of the enjoyment of doing the exercise now rather than future health gains.","[M] These findings suggest that when it comes to achieving your goals, enjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term benefits. In other words, present self trumps (战胜) future self. Who says instant gratification has to be a bad thing? By all means, set ambitious long-term goals for your future self, but when it comes to actually following through day-to-day, make sure your present self knows what''s in it for her too."]','["Hal Hershfield, a psychologist at the UCLA Anderson School of Management, wanted to know why people weren''t saving for retirement. Across the board, people are living longer. Logically, they''ll need more money to live comfortably in their post-work years. And yet, savings rates in the U.S. have gone down in recent decades, not up.","To help explain this seemingly irrational behavior, Hershfield and his team scanned the brains of study participants while asking them to what degree various traits (特征)—like “honorable” or “funny”—applied to their current self, their future self, a current other, or a future other. As participants answered, Hershfield''s team recorded which parts of their brains lit up. Unsurprisingly, people''s brains were most active when thinking about their current selves and least active when thinking about a current other. But the team found that participants'' brain activity while considering their future selves more closely resembled their brain activity while thinking about a current other rather than the current self.","Put in practical terms, when thinking of yourself in a month or a year or a decade, your brain registers that person in ways similar to how it would register Taylor Swift or the mailman. Understood in that way, saving for retirement is the equivalent of giving money away to someone else entirely","In light of Hershfield''s study, one simple question arises: Is it possible to make our present selves give a damn about our future selves? The answers are anything but simple.","Seen through the lens of the present self versus the future self, our self-defeating actions—like choosing to watch television rather than go to the gym—suddenly make perfect sense. We get to enjoy the very concrete, immediate benefits of our actions while someone else (namely, our future selves) suffers the hypothetical (假设的), far-off consequences. As a result, the decisions we make for our present selves often look very different from our decisions for our future selves. We believe that tomorrow. will be different. We believe that we will be different tomorrow; but in doing so, we prioritize our current mood over the consequences of our inaction for the future self.","Understanding our procrastination (拖延) through the lens of the present and future selves, we''re left with three possible solutions: The first is to force your future self to do whatever your present self doesn''t want to do. The second is to convince your present self that your future self is, in fact, still you. If the central problem is that we think of our future selves as other people, it follows that trying to identify more closely with our future selves will encourage us to make better long-term decisions.","In a follow-up study, Hershfield wanted to explore ways to bridge the disconnect between the present and future selves and encourage people to save more for retirement. He and his team took photos of study participants, and then used image processing to visually age their faces. Participants were then placed in a virtual reality setting where they could look into a mirror and see their aged selves looking back at them. Participants who saw their aged selves said they would save 30% more of their salary for retirement than the control group.","Whatever your long-term goals may be—getting in better shape, launching your own business, writing a book—thinking about your deadline in terms of days rather than months or years can help you wrap your mind around how close the future really is.","The third solution is to forget about your future self and use your present self''s love of instant gratifcation (满足感) to your advantage. While the two tactics (手段) above can be effective in making better long-term choices, in the end, you''re still struggling against human nature. Our brains are hard-wired for instant gratification. Instead of fighting your present self''s need for immediate rewards, why not use it to your advantage? When most of us set goals, we focus on long-term results we want to see— e.g., losing weight, getting a promotion, retiring in comfort, etc. While those visions of our future selves can be inspiring, when it comes to actually doing the day-to-day work, it may be more effective to reframe activities in terms of their immediate, or at least very near-term, rewards.","Take writing this article, for instance. It''s easy for me to imagine how amazing it will feel at the end of the workday to have this article done. This isn''t just my opinion. Research partners Kaitlin Woolley of Cornell University and Ayelet Fishbach of the University of Chicago have made a career out of studying the differences between the goals that people achieve and the ones that fall to the wayside","“In one study, we asked people online about the goals they set at the beginning of the year. Most people set goals to achieve delayed, long-term benefits, such as career advancement, debt repayment, or improved health. We asked these individuals how enjoyable it was to pursue their goal, as well as how important their goal was. We also asked whether they were still working on their goals two months after setting them. We found that enjoyment predicted people''s goal persistence two months after setting the goal far more than how important they rated their goal to be,” Woolley said.","This pattern held true across a wide variety of goals from exercising to studying to eating healthier foods For example, people ate 50% more of a healthy food when directed to focus on the good taste rather than the long-term health benefits. Other studies have shown a greater uptake of exercise in people who were told to think of the enjoyment of doing the exercise now rather than future health gains.","These findings suggest that when it comes to achieving your goals, enjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term benefits. In other words, present self trumps (战胜) future self. Who says instant gratification has to be a bad thing? By all means, set ambitious long-term goals for your future self, but when it comes to actually following through day-to-day, make sure your present self knows what''s in it for her too."]') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,kind=EXCLUDED.kind,title=EXCLUDED.title,instructions=EXCLUDED.instructions,paragraphs_json=EXCLUDED.paragraphs_json,study_paragraphs_json=EXCLUDED.study_paragraphs_json;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-36','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',0,'choice','36. Our brains are genetically determined to satisfy immediate desires.','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','I','参考答案 I，依据原文整理。I 段说大脑天生倾向即时满足，并建议利用眼前奖励促进长期目标。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-37','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',1,'choice','37. Taken in a practical way, saving for post-work years is like giving money away to others','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','C','参考答案 C，依据原文整理。C 段把为退休储蓄比作把钱送给另一个人，因为大脑将未来的自己视为陌生人。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-38','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',2,'choice','38. Research found that, as regards achievement of one''s goals, it is important to focus more on enjoying the process than the long-term benefits','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','M','参考答案 M，依据原文整理。M 段概括研究结论：享受实现目标的过程比想要长期收益更重要。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-39','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',3,'choice','39. Regarding our future selves as still being ourselves will help us make better long-term decisions','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','F','参考答案 F，依据原文整理。F 段的第二种解决方法是认识到未来的自己仍是自己，这有助于作出更好的长期决策。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-40','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',4,'choice','40. Savings rates in America have dropped in recent decades even though people''s life expectancy has increased.','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','A','参考答案 A，依据原文整理。A 段指出人们寿命延长，但美国近几十年的储蓄率反而下降。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-41','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',5,'choice','41. Researchers found that enjoyment rather than importance enabled people to persist in their goals.','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','K','参考答案 K，依据原文整理。K 段研究发现，追求目标时的乐趣比目标的重要性更能预测两个月后的坚持程度。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-42','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',6,'choice','42. When making decisions, we give priority to our current frame of mind without thinking much of the consequences.','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','E','参考答案 E，依据原文整理。E 段说人们优先考虑当前心情，忽略不行动对未来自己的后果。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-43','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',7,'choice','43. People ate more of a healthy food when they focused on its good taste instead of its long-term benefits','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','L','参考答案 L，依据原文整理。L 段举例，关注健康食物好味道的人比关注长期健康收益的人多吃了 50%。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-44','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',8,'choice','44. As was expected, when people thought of their present selves, their brains were observed to become more active','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','B','参考答案 B，依据原文整理。B 段脑部扫描结果显示，思考当前自己时的脑活动最强，符合研究者预期。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-q-45','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3',9,'choice','45. Researchers found that participants who saw the images of their aged selves would save more for their later years than those who didn''t.','[{"key":"A","text":"段落 A"},{"key":"B","text":"段落 B"},{"key":"C","text":"段落 C"},{"key":"D","text":"段落 D"},{"key":"E","text":"段落 E"},{"key":"F","text":"段落 F"},{"key":"G","text":"段落 G"},{"key":"H","text":"段落 H"},{"key":"I","text":"段落 I"},{"key":"J","text":"段落 J"},{"key":"K","text":"段落 K"},{"key":"L","text":"段落 L"},{"key":"M","text":"段落 M"}]','G','参考答案 G，依据原文整理。G 段用虚拟现实展示参与者变老后的样貌，看到老年自己的人表示愿为退休多存 30% 的工资。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-1-1','cet4-2024-06-2',200,0,'Hal Hershfield, a psychologist at the UCLA Anderson School of Management, wanted to know why people weren''t saving for retirement.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-1-2','cet4-2024-06-2',200,1,'Across the board, people are living longer.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-1-3','cet4-2024-06-2',200,2,'Logically, they''ll need more money to live comfortably in their post-work years.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-1-4','cet4-2024-06-2',200,3,'And yet, savings rates in the U.S. have gone down in recent decades, not up.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-2-1','cet4-2024-06-2',201,0,'To help explain this seemingly irrational behavior, Hershfield and his team scanned the brains of study participants while asking them to what degree various traits (特征)—like “honorable” or “funny”—applied to their current self, their future self, a current other, or a future other.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-2-2','cet4-2024-06-2',201,1,'As participants answered, Hershfield''s team recorded which parts of their brains lit up.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-2-3','cet4-2024-06-2',201,2,'Unsurprisingly, people''s brains were most active when thinking about their current selves and least active when thinking about a current other.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-2-4','cet4-2024-06-2',201,3,'But the team found that participants'' brain activity while considering their future selves more closely resembled their brain activity while thinking about a current other rather than the current self.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-3-1','cet4-2024-06-2',202,0,'Put in practical terms, when thinking of yourself in a month or a year or a decade, your brain registers that person in ways similar to how it would register Taylor Swift or the mailman.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-3-2','cet4-2024-06-2',202,1,'Understood in that way, saving for retirement is the equivalent of giving money away to someone else entirely','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-4-1','cet4-2024-06-2',203,0,'In light of Hershfield''s study, one simple question arises: Is it possible to make our present selves give a damn about our future selves?','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-4-2','cet4-2024-06-2',203,1,'The answers are anything but simple.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-5-1','cet4-2024-06-2',204,0,'Seen through the lens of the present self versus the future self, our self-defeating actions—like choosing to watch television rather than go to the gym—suddenly make perfect sense.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-5-2','cet4-2024-06-2',204,1,'We get to enjoy the very concrete, immediate benefits of our actions while someone else (namely, our future selves) suffers the hypothetical (假设的), far-off consequences.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-5-3','cet4-2024-06-2',204,2,'As a result, the decisions we make for our present selves often look very different from our decisions for our future selves.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-5-4','cet4-2024-06-2',204,3,'We believe that tomorrow. will be different.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-5-5','cet4-2024-06-2',204,4,'We believe that we will be different tomorrow; but in doing so, we prioritize our current mood over the consequences of our inaction for the future self.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-6-1','cet4-2024-06-2',205,0,'Understanding our procrastination (拖延) through the lens of the present and future selves, we''re left with three possible solutions: The first is to force your future self to do whatever your present self doesn''t want to do.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-6-2','cet4-2024-06-2',205,1,'The second is to convince your present self that your future self is, in fact, still you.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-6-3','cet4-2024-06-2',205,2,'If the central problem is that we think of our future selves as other people, it follows that trying to identify more closely with our future selves will encourage us to make better long-term decisions.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-7-1','cet4-2024-06-2',206,0,'In a follow-up study, Hershfield wanted to explore ways to bridge the disconnect between the present and future selves and encourage people to save more for retirement.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-7-2','cet4-2024-06-2',206,1,'He and his team took photos of study participants, and then used image processing to visually age their faces.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-7-3','cet4-2024-06-2',206,2,'Participants were then placed in a virtual reality setting where they could look into a mirror and see their aged selves looking back at them.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-7-4','cet4-2024-06-2',206,3,'Participants who saw their aged selves said they would save 30% more of their salary for retirement than the control group.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-8-1','cet4-2024-06-2',207,0,'Whatever your long-term goals may be—getting in better shape, launching your own business, writing a book—thinking about your deadline in terms of days rather than months or years can help you wrap your mind around how close the future really is.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-9-1','cet4-2024-06-2',208,0,'The third solution is to forget about your future self and use your present self''s love of instant gratifcation (满足感) to your advantage.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-9-2','cet4-2024-06-2',208,1,'While the two tactics (手段) above can be effective in making better long-term choices, in the end, you''re still struggling against human nature.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-9-3','cet4-2024-06-2',208,2,'Our brains are hard-wired for instant gratification.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-9-4','cet4-2024-06-2',208,3,'Instead of fighting your present self''s need for immediate rewards, why not use it to your advantage?','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-9-5','cet4-2024-06-2',208,4,'When most of us set goals, we focus on long-term results we want to see— e.g., losing weight, getting a promotion, retiring in comfort, etc.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-9-6','cet4-2024-06-2',208,5,'While those visions of our future selves can be inspiring, when it comes to actually doing the day-to-day work, it may be more effective to reframe activities in terms of their immediate, or at least very near-term, rewards.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-10-1','cet4-2024-06-2',209,0,'Take writing this article, for instance.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-10-2','cet4-2024-06-2',209,1,'It''s easy for me to imagine how amazing it will feel at the end of the workday to have this article done.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-10-3','cet4-2024-06-2',209,2,'This isn''t just my opinion.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-10-4','cet4-2024-06-2',209,3,'Research partners Kaitlin Woolley of Cornell University and Ayelet Fishbach of the University of Chicago have made a career out of studying the differences between the goals that people achieve and the ones that fall to the wayside','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-11-1','cet4-2024-06-2',210,0,'“In one study, we asked people online about the goals they set at the beginning of the year.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-11-2','cet4-2024-06-2',210,1,'Most people set goals to achieve delayed, long-term benefits, such as career advancement, debt repayment, or improved health.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-11-3','cet4-2024-06-2',210,2,'We asked these individuals how enjoyable it was to pursue their goal, as well as how important their goal was.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-11-4','cet4-2024-06-2',210,3,'We also asked whether they were still working on their goals two months after setting them.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-11-5','cet4-2024-06-2',210,4,'We found that enjoyment predicted people''s goal persistence two months after setting the goal far more than how important they rated their goal to be,” Woolley said.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-12-1','cet4-2024-06-2',211,0,'This pattern held true across a wide variety of goals from exercising to studying to eating healthier foods For example, people ate 50% more of a healthy food when directed to focus on the good taste rather than the long-term health benefits.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-12-2','cet4-2024-06-2',211,1,'Other studies have shown a greater uptake of exercise in people who were told to think of the enjoyment of doing the exercise now rather than future health gains.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-13-1','cet4-2024-06-2',212,0,'These findings suggest that when it comes to achieving your goals, enjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term benefits.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-13-2','cet4-2024-06-2',212,1,'In other words, present self trumps (战胜) future self.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-13-3','cet4-2024-06-2',212,2,'Who says instant gratification has to be a bad thing?','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO sentences (id,paper_id,paragraph_index,sentence_index,source_text,section_id) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-3-sentence-13-4','cet4-2024-06-2',212,3,'By all means, set ambitious long-term goals for your future self, but when it comes to actually following through day-to-day, make sure your present self knows what''s in it for her too.','cet4-2024-06-2-section-3') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET paragraph_index=EXCLUDED.paragraph_index,sentence_index=EXCLUDED.sentence_index,source_text=EXCLUDED.source_text,section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id;
INSERT INTO paper_sections (id,paper_id,position,kind,title,instructions,paragraphs_json,study_paragraphs_json) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-4','cet4-2024-06-2',3,'reading','仔细阅读 · Passage One（46–50）','There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C) and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre','["People often wonder why some entrepreneurs have greater success than others. Is it habits, connections, luck, work ethic or any other behavior? I believe the key to success is willpower. Willpower is the ability to control yourself. It is a strong determination that allows you to do something difficult. It is a behavior we are born with more than one we learn; however, it is possible to not only learn it, but also strengthen it with constant exercise.","Willpower is just like a muscle; to keep it strong you need to constantly exercise it. People with a great amount of willpower have the discipline to develop positive, successful habits. Even with an incredible amount of talent, without the discipline and motivation to create positive habits, it can be difficult to achieve success.","Willpower and habits go hand in hand. It is critical to create good habits and take the actions necessary to stick to those habits day in and day out for greater success. Almost half of our daily actions are part of our habits and not decisions, so once the correct habits are in place, you will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to-day basis.","The best way to create and stick to a habit is to have strong motivation. It''s easier to change your habits to lose weight if you have a health issue and you want to be around longer for your kids, or if your business is something you''re passionate about. Having a valuable outcome associated with a habit will help you stick to that habit permanently.","The art of self-control is one that most successful individuals have mastered. Self-control enables you to avoid behaviors that don''t contribute to your success and adopt those that do.","Because there is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control, it can be easy to get off track. However if you work on sticking to those small positive habits one day at a time, it becomes easier to stay strong and achieve that delayed reward. Once a reward is achieved, it is much easier to continue sticking to your habits"]','["People often wonder why some entrepreneurs have greater success than others. Is it habits, connections, luck, work ethic or any other behavior? I believe the key to success is willpower. Willpower is the ability to control yourself. It is a strong determination that allows you to do something difficult. It is a behavior we are born with more than one we learn; however, it is possible to not only learn it, but also strengthen it with constant exercise.","Willpower is just like a muscle; to keep it strong you need to constantly exercise it. People with a great amount of willpower have the discipline to develop positive, successful habits. Even with an incredible amount of talent, without the discipline and motivation to create positive habits, it can be difficult to achieve success.","Willpower and habits go hand in hand. It is critical to create good habits and take the actions necessary to stick to those habits day in and day out for greater success. Almost half of our daily actions are part of our habits and not decisions, so once the correct habits are in place, you will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to-day basis.","The best way to create and stick to a habit is to have strong motivation. It''s easier to change your habits to lose weight if you have a health issue and you want to be around longer for your kids, or if your business is something you''re passionate about. Having a valuable outcome associated with a habit will help you stick to that habit permanently.","The art of self-control is one that most successful individuals have mastered. Self-control enables you to avoid behaviors that don''t contribute to your success and adopt those that do.","Because there is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control, it can be easy to get off track. However if you work on sticking to those small positive habits one day at a time, it becomes easier to stay strong and achieve that delayed reward. Once a reward is achieved, it is much easier to continue sticking to your habits"]') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,kind=EXCLUDED.kind,title=EXCLUDED.title,instructions=EXCLUDED.instructions,paragraphs_json=EXCLUDED.paragraphs_json,study_paragraphs_json=EXCLUDED.study_paragraphs_json;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-4-q-46','cet4-2024-06-2-section-4',0,'choice','46. What does the author say we need to do to strengthen our willpower?','[{"key":"A","text":"Keep it under control."},{"key":"B","text":"Apply it continuously."},{"key":"C","text":"Learn from entrepreneurs"},{"key":"D","text":"Aim at success determinedly."}]','B','参考答案 B，依据原文整理。首段说意志力可以通过 constant exercise 增强；第二段用肌肉作比，强调持续锻炼。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-4-q-47','cet4-2024-06-2-section-4',1,'choice','47. How are almost half of our daily actions performed according to the passage?','[{"key":"A","text":"Out of habit."},{"key":"B","text":"With determination."},{"key":"C","text":"Like muscle building."},{"key":"D","text":"By self-discipline"}]','A','参考答案 A，依据原文整理。第三段明确说日常行为近一半源于习惯，而不是临时作出的决定。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-4-q-48','cet4-2024-06-2-section-4',2,'choice','48. What will help people stick to doing something constructive automatically?','[{"key":"A","text":"Practising it on a day-to-day basis"},{"key":"B","text":"Associating it with improving health."},{"key":"C","text":"Possessing a reasonable amount of talent"},{"key":"D","text":"Foreseeing the desired outcome it will yield."}]','D','参考答案 D，依据原文整理。第四段说强烈动机和与习惯相关的有价值结果，有助于长期坚持有益习惯。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-4-q-49','cet4-2024-06-2-section-4',3,'choice','49. How does the art of self-control help us succeed?','[{"key":"A","text":"By allowing us to remain clear-headed permanently."},{"key":"B","text":"By enabling us to alter our behaviors constantly."},{"key":"C","text":"By enabling us to take positive actions"},{"key":"D","text":"By allowing us to avoid taking risks."}]','C','参考答案 C，依据原文整理。第五段说自控使人避免无助于成功的行为，并采用能促进成功的行为。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
INSERT INTO questions (id,section_id,position,type,prompt,options_json,answer_text,explanation,points) VALUES ('cet4-2024-06-2-section-4-q-50','cet4-2024-06-2-section-4',4,'choice','50. Why can it be difficult for us to maintain self-control?','[{"key":"A","text":"Most of us are not in the habit of exercising self-control."},{"key":"B","text":"We may not get immediate rewards from self-control."},{"key":"C","text":"Self-control tends to be associated with pains."},{"key":"D","text":"Self-control only brings about small benefits"}]','B','参考答案 B，依据原文整理。最后一段指出自控与 delayed satisfaction 相联系，无法立即获得回报容易使人偏离目标。',2) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET position=EXCLUDED.position,type=EXCLUDED.type,prompt=EXCLUDED.prompt,options_json=EXCLUDED.options_json,answer_text=EXCLUDED.answer_text,explanation=EXCLUDED.explanation,points=EXCLUDED.points;
